00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:06,599
Chapter 7 Goal Misgeneralization Section
7.1 Introduction this chapter

2
00:00:06,599 --> 00:00:10,720
dives into the phenomenon of goal
misgeneralization, perhaps the

3
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:15,919
most counterintuitive problem in AI
safety. Unlike specification problems

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,359
where we simply fail to provide the right
training

5
00:00:18,359 --> 00:00:21,920
signal or capability failures where
systems cannot do what

6
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:26,920
we want, goal misgeneralization occurs
when systems internalize different

7
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:31,649
behaviors than intended despite receiving
correct training signals. We

8
00:00:31,649 --> 00:00:35,049
start by establishing what the problem is,
explaining why

9
00:00:35,049 --> 00:00:38,289
it happens. Then we look at concerning
manifestations of

10
00:00:38,289 --> 00:00:41,609
it like scheming, and finally go through
some detection

11
00:00:41,609 --> 00:00:45,329
and mitigation approaches. AI systems can
learn different goals

12
00:00:45,329 --> 00:00:48,770
than what we intended, even when their
training appears

13
00:00:48,770 --> 00:00:53,530
completely successful. The first section
explains why generalization should

14
00:00:53,530 --> 00:00:56,530
not be treated as one dimensional. This is
similar

15
00:00:56,530 --> 00:00:59,369
to the nuance we pointed to in the
Evaluations

16
00:00:59,369 --> 00:01:03,009
chapter. Capabilities measure what a model
can do, and

17
00:01:03,009 --> 00:01:05,209
goals measure what a model is trying to
do.

18
00:01:05,609 --> 00:01:09,930
They can generalize independently,
creating scenarios where systems retain

19
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:14,250
sophisticated abilities while pursuing
entirely different objectives than
intended.

20
00:01:15,290 --> 00:01:18,969
Multiple different goals can produce
identical behavior during training,

21
00:01:19,450 --> 00:01:23,450
making them behaviorally indistinguishable
until deployment reveals which goal

22
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:27,010
the system actually learned. Learning
dynamics help us understand

23
00:01:27,329 --> 00:01:31,409
how identical training signals can produce
different learned algorithms.

24
00:01:31,890 --> 00:01:35,250
When we train neural networks, we're not
directly installing

25
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:39,530
goals. We're creating selection pressures
guided by our training

26
00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:43,250
signals that favor certain behaviors over
others. There are

27
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:46,730
several questions to explore. What space
is the training

28
00:01:46,730 --> 00:01:49,609
signal guiding the model through? Can we
shape the

29
00:01:49,609 --> 00:01:53,739
space somehow? These are called loss
landscapes. Their geometry,

30
00:01:53,739 --> 00:01:58,260
path dependence from random
initialization, and inductive biases like

31
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:04,060
simplicity systematically determine which
algorithmic solutions get discovered.
Understanding

32
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:06,978
these dynamics helps us figure out which
algorithms get

33
00:02:06,978 --> 00:02:10,219
learned during training, which in turn
determines the goals

34
00:02:10,219 --> 00:02:13,900
of the final model we deploy. Goal
misgeneralization becomes

35
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:19,250
increasingly concerning as systems develop
sophisticated goal directedness. Even

36
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:22,250
though multiple algorithms that display
the same behavior at

37
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:24,770
the end of training are possible, not all
of

38
00:02:24,770 --> 00:02:28,409
them are concerning. The goal directedness
section starts to

39
00:02:28,409 --> 00:02:33,169
look at when behaviorally
indistinguishable models become dangerous.
Models

40
00:02:33,169 --> 00:02:35,490
can have degrees to which they are goal
directed,

41
00:02:35,650 --> 00:02:39,090
ranging from really complex pattern
matching and learned heuristics

42
00:02:39,330 --> 00:02:44,090
to systems implementing genuine internal
optimization processes. The higher

43
00:02:44,090 --> 00:02:46,960
the degree of goal directedness a system
develops, the

44
00:02:47,189 --> 00:02:51,229
the more concerning goal misgeneralization
becomes, as these systems

45
00:02:51,229 --> 00:02:55,430
might systematically pursue objectives
across diverse contexts and obstacles.

46
00:02:55,750 --> 00:03:00,110
Sophisticated goal directedness leads to
goal preservation behavior which

47
00:03:00,110 --> 00:03:03,509
can result in scheming. The most dangerous
form occurs

48
00:03:03,509 --> 00:03:07,430
when goal directed systems develop
situational awareness about their

49
00:03:07,430 --> 00:03:11,750
training process and long term planning
capabilities. They might

50
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:15,800
choose to strategically conceal misaligned
objectives to preserve their

51
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,319
true goals until there's no longer a
threat of

52
00:03:18,319 --> 00:03:22,680
modification. We walk you through
empirical evidence from demonstrations

53
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:27,520
of alignment, faking in context scheming,
and agentic misalignment

54
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:29,960
to highlight that this type of outcome is
possible.

55
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:32,639
Then we also look at both sides of the

56
00:03:32,639 --> 00:03:35,840
argument for the likelihood of whether
scheming behavior would

57
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,560
likely arise as a consequence of the
machine learning

58
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:41,879
process. The rest of the chapter focuses
on answering

59
00:03:41,879 --> 00:03:45,319
the question what can we do about this?
The

60
00:03:45,319 --> 00:03:47,840
next few sections serve as the links
between goal

61
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:53,120
mis generation, evaluations and
interpretability. Chapters Detection
methods focus

62
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:57,199
on discovering behaviors like goal
directedness, goal preservation, and

63
00:03:57,199 --> 00:04:01,599
scheming. Building on techniques from the
evaluations chapter, we

64
00:04:01,599 --> 00:04:05,560
examine behavioral methods that monitor
external reasoning traces and

65
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:11,069
interpretability techniques like linear
probes, sparse autoencoders, and
activation

66
00:04:11,069 --> 00:04:14,629
manipulation. There are a lot of different
capabilities and

67
00:04:14,629 --> 00:04:18,910
combinations of capabilities to test
mitigations focus on preventing

68
00:04:18,910 --> 00:04:23,829
and correcting goal misgeneralization.
Across the development pipeline, we

69
00:04:23,829 --> 00:04:28,230
explore training time interventions like
adversarial training and curriculum

70
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:32,149
learning post training techniques like
steering vectors and model

71
00:04:32,149 --> 00:04:36,550
editing and deployment time safeguards
including runtime monitoring and

72
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:40,680
sandboxing. As with all AI safety
challenges explored in

73
00:04:40,680 --> 00:04:43,519
this book, we advocate for a defense in
depth

74
00:04:43,519 --> 00:04:47,199
approach where multiple detection and
mitigation measures are layered

75
00:04:47,199 --> 00:04:50,600
to provide robust defenses against goal
misgeneralization.
